// Every robot of a stack as one MCP surface: who each robot is, its posture,
// arm and gripper moves, its mobile base's moves and the stop that ends them,
// its limbs' live state, its base's odometry,
// its collision readout, the health and alerts of its motor-bearing
// components, its cameras with their frames, controls and geometry, where
// it carries its sensors and where each of its frames stands, where its
// design lets it work, its brain, its recorder, its motion planner and its
// navigator, with the navigator's maps of rooms and the base's place in
// them, each addressed by the robot's name on one endpoint, and the record
// of the calls that changed a robot's state.
//
// The document is written against contracts, never against a model. Each
// target below is one contract a robot may bring, and the listing tool
// names the tools and the resources a robot's targets give it. The same
// document is served on the physical fleet and in every simulation, so
// everything a model does here transfers between them.
//
// `robots` makes it a per-robot surface: every target is a set the stack's
// robots fill, a join adding its robot's instances and a removal taking
// them out while the server runs, and every tool takes `robot`, the name
// robot.list reports. A target with an `argument` is filled any number of
// times per robot, once per camera or per motor-bearing component, and a
// call or a resource on it names that member too.
//
// A launcher deploys it once per stack under
// `source: { exposures: ["robot_control:v1"] }` with no `links`: the
// fragments a robot's copy runs add its instances to the targets with
// `add_links`, the launchers hub's `robot_control` option. The server built into
// peppy serves it at /robot_control/v1/mcp.
{
  peppy_schema: "mcp_exposure/v1",
  manifest: { name: "robot_control", tag: "v1" },

  // Advertised to clients through `server/discover`. `instructions` is the
  // prose a model reads before it decides what to call: what every robot
  // shares. What differs between robots is what robot.list answers. A client
  // can read this endpoint alone, so no text names another endpoint or a
  // tool of one: test_robot_endpoint_names_no_other_endpoint.py.
  server: {
    title: "Robots",
    instructions: "Every robot of the stack, drivable by name on this one endpoint, whatever its model. Call robot.list first: for each robot present it reports the tools that robot answers under tools: the robot. tools, and the brain., recorder., planner. and navigator. tools of its brain, its recorder, its motion planner and its navigator where it has them; the resources it publishes under resources, each read at peppy://resource/<name>; its camera names and its motor-bearing components under members; its model and the machine hosting it under identity; and its arm and gripper names, with the joints behind each arm, under limbs. Every other tool takes robot, one of the names robot.list reports, and a camera tool takes camera too, one of the names the listing gives that robot under camera or depth_camera in members. A robot's motor health and alerts are resources, one of each per motor-bearing component, listed under its resources with the component's name in them. A call naming a robot that is not there, a tool that robot does not answer, or a camera it does not have is refused, and the refusal names what is there. A call that the robot refuses, or that fails, gives outcome, failure_kind and message, as the structured content of a tool error, or as the data of the error of a failed task: outcome is refused for a goal the robot refused before it ran, and failed for a goal that ran and failed or a request the robot answered with an error; failure_kind names the cause, one of attempt_failed, timeout, precondition_failed, resource_unavailable and rejected, which every robot gives, or a kind of the tool: unreachable, no plan reaches the target of a move, or a robot's collision guard would hold a path short, or the goal of a navigation stands in collision with an obstacle the robot's sensors have seen or its loaded map holds; no_path, the planner found positions that meet the goal but no path to them, or no path joins the base to the goal of a navigation around the obstacles after its recoveries; start_in_collision, the start of a plan touches an obstacle or the robot's own body, or stands nearer than the clearance to the map or an object, or the base stands in collision with an obstacle as a navigation starts or plans; blocked, the moving base found no way forward: a navigation after its recoveries, or a navigator.spin, navigator.drive_on_heading or navigator.back_up whose sweep meets an obstacle; not_localized, a navigation whose target is in map has no loaded map, or the base is not localized in it; leader_streaming, a leader such as a leader arm or a teleoperation headset streams to a limb of the call, or a leader on drive_link streams to the base of the call; not_found, brain.identify_item finds no item that matches; no_match, no scan matched the loaded map within the search of navigator.set_initial_pose and the matching's limits; unknown_frame, no_chain, extrapolation and broken_tree, a frame or a time of the call that the robot's tree cannot place, as the frame rule says; and message says why in the robot's words. Such a call gives no result. A move that a cancel or robot.stop ends is cancelled: inside a call it ends with a tool error whose text gives the reason and whose structured content is the result, measured when the move ended; as a task it ends cancelled, and its status message gives both. robot.stop reaches a navigator goal through the base, so a stop during a silence of the base's motion sensing ends the goal with resource_unavailable. brain.grab_item, brain.drop_item, brain.place_item and brain.abort can also complete with success false in their result, and message then says why the sequence did not run to its end. This is the robots' own surface: what a robot cannot do through these tools, it cannot do. Every limb tool takes the arm and gripper names the listing reports under limbs. A robot that is a mobile base, such as botshare_base, has no limbs: robot.list reports its limbs as null, with a note under notes. robot.move_base moves it to a target pose, which the robot places in odom, and robot.drive_base drives it at a velocity for a time, which the robot places in the robot frame, each by the frame rule when the robot takes the call; both give final_pose, where the base's odometry placed the robot frame in odom when the move ended. odom is the robot frame where the base stood when its odometry started, fixed to the floor, and it starts over with the odometry, which robot.base_state then reports with a new odometry_start_timestamp. The base says what it is and how fast it may move with robot.describe_base, and it reports where its odometry places it on the robot.base_state resource. One move per base at a time: a base move while another runs, or while the base still comes to rest from one, is refused with precondition_failed, or with leader_streaming while the navigator drives the base, and a navigator goal replaces the navigator's goal that runs; a base move is refused with resource_unavailable while the base's motion sensing is silent, once its newest measurement is more than 0.5 s old: retry once the base reports its odometry. Before the base first reports, its tree holds no odom, so a robot.move_base, and any base move given in odom, is refused with unknown_frame: retry once robot.base_state reports. robot.stop ends a base's moves too. A robot whose mobile base measures its odometry and carries a 2-D lidar fixed to it can have a navigator that maps rooms with that lidar, saves each map under a name and keeps the base localized in the loaded map: its map tools, navigator.get_map_state, navigator.start_mapping, navigator.finish_mapping, navigator.list_maps, navigator.remove_map, navigator.load_map, navigator.set_initial_pose and navigator.reinitialize_global_localization, move nothing. To map a room, call navigator.start_mapping, drive the base around the room with robot.move_base or robot.drive_base, then call navigator.finish_mapping, which saves the map. After the navigator restarts, no map is loaded: call navigator.load_map, then navigator.set_initial_pose with where the robot stands in that map. To localize a base whose place in the loaded map is unknown, call navigator.reinitialize_global_localization and turn the base in place with robot.drive_base until the navigator.map_pose resource reports localized true. Map rule: the loaded map is the map being built while mapping runs, and otherwise the map that navigator.load_map or navigator.finish_mapping loaded last, until navigator.remove_map removes it; there is at most one, and none when the navigator starts. The map frame of a map is odom as it stood when that map's mapping started, fixed to the floor of the room from then on, and the map frame of the loaded map is map in the robot's tree: every pose of navigator.set_initial_pose and of the navigator.map_pose resource lies in map, its position [x, y, 0] and its orientation turning about +z alone, and navigator.get_map_state gives the extent of the loaded map's grid in it. The navigator has an estimate of where the robot frame stands in the loaded map from the moment mapping starts, navigator.set_initial_pose matches a scan or navigator.reinitialize_global_localization starts a search, until a navigator.load_map, a navigator.start_mapping or a navigator.remove_map of the loaded map. The base is localized while mapping runs, and otherwise while that estimate's standard deviations lie within the navigator's limits: the search's limits while a search holds the estimate and for the update with which it localizes the base, and the matching's limits for every later update. A base moved within its map without driving keeps its estimate, which then stands where the base stood before, and stays localized while that estimate's deviations lie within the matching's limits, until navigator.set_initial_pose matches or a search localizes it. A map name is a lowercase letter, then lowercase letters, digits and _, at most 64 characters. Maps are saved on the machine that runs the navigator and last across its restarts; a map tool that reads or writes the saved maps fails with attempt_failed, the message naming the cause, when the directory of the maps cannot be read or written. navigator.start_mapping, navigator.set_initial_pose and navigator.reinitialize_global_localization test the base's odometry_source, as robot.describe_base names it, which the navigator reads each time robot.base_state reports a new odometry_start_timestamp, the first included: such a call made before the base first reports, or before that read answers, waits for it up to the navigator's source timeout, and is refused with resource_unavailable, before any other check, when the read fails or that timeout passes first. A call of a map tool is then checked in this order: rejected for a value the tool does not take, precondition_failed for a state the tool names, then resource_unavailable; the message names the cause and what to do, a precondition_failed one the state that gives it, and one given while no map is loaded names navigator.load_map. Distances are meters, durations seconds, angles radians. A position is [x, y, z] in meters and an orientation a unit quaternion [x, y, z, w]. A pose of a limb tool or a brain tool is the gripper's grasp point. The grasp point is midway between the finger pads; so101 reports a fixed point near its jaws instead. The robot frame is fixed to the robot's base: its origin is where the robot's URDF puts its root link, on the floor under the base for a mobile base's base_footprint, and its axes are a body's as REP 103 gives them, +X the way the robot faces, +Y to its left and +Z up; it moves with the robot and is not a frame of the room or of a simulated world. On a wheeled base its origin is on the floor at the point the base turns about in place, midway between the driven wheels of a differential base. The robot frame is the root link of the robot's URDF, and robot.describe_sensors gives its name in robot_frame, such as base_link. Frame rule: every point and pose a tool takes or gives names its frame and its timestamp, in its own record beside its position and its orientation, or in the record that holds it. frame is a frame of the robot's tree, as robot.lookup_transform places one: the robot frame, a link that planner.describe_arms names, a sensor frame that robot.describe_sensors gives, odom on a mobile base, or map on a mobile base whose navigator maps rooms, by the map rule. A call may leave frame out, apart from the target of navigator.set_initial_pose, which names map: a point or pose with no frame lies in the robot frame, and an answer always names its frame. timestamp is an RFC 3339 time on the robot's clock, the clock of the capture timestamps of its cameras: a call may leave timestamp out for the latest time, and an answer gives it, apart from values that hold at every time, which leave it out. The robot places each point and pose of a call by its tree at the call's timestamp, once, when it takes the call: a pose with no timestamp is placed by the latest transforms when the robot takes the call, and one in the robot frame names where the robot frame stands then; a pose given with the timestamp of an answer names where its frame stood at that time, so a pose read before the robot moved names the same place after. A tool says where it then holds what it placed: against a link that the move moves, or in the root of a frame's chain. A call whose frame or time the robot cannot place gives, before anything moves, unknown_frame, a frame that the robot's tree does not name; no_chain, a frame of the call and the frame the robot places it in are in trees that share no frame; extrapolation, the tree holds no value of a moving part at a timestamp of the call, a time before the oldest it keeps or after the newest; or broken_tree, two of the robot's nodes place one of its frames, or its chain runs through a cycle; and message names the cause. An orientation whose squared norm lies 0.01 or more from 1 is refused with rejected, and the robot normalizes each orientation it takes; a call leaves out an orientation to name none where a tool says what that means. Every answer names the frame and the time of its points and poses, and each tool says which they are. A velocity follows the same rule: a velocity of a call names its frame and its timestamp, the velocity of the robot frame given in that frame, linear_velocity [x, y, z] in m/s and angular_velocity [x, y, z] in rad/s, turning counterclockwise about each axis, and a velocity with no frame lies in the robot frame. Call robot.move_to_ready before any robot.move_arm; the rest posture is not a place to plan Cartesian moves from. One motion per limb at a time: a goal for a limb that is still moving is refused with precondition_failed, and a goal for a limb that a leader streams to is refused with leader_streaming. No move of the robot. tools checks the floor, a table, an object or an item a gripper holds, and no base move of them checks an obstacle; the moves of the navigator. tools drive a base around what the robot's sensors see; on a robot with a motion planner, plan a path around what its depth camera has seen and the objects of its planning scene with planner.plan_joint_path and run it with robot.execute_joint_path; without a depth camera, a plan keeps clear of the robot's own body and the objects of its planning scene only. so101 has no depth camera, so its plan keeps clear of its own links and the objects a client enters alone; folded onto itself as it rests, its arm refuses a plan with start_in_collision: move it to robot.move_to_home, out of contact, and plan from there. Five joints end on few orientations exactly: a pose robot.move_arm refuses for its orientation on so101 plans with planner.plan_joint_path, its orientation constraint naming the slack with tolerance_rad or free_axis. A robot with a collision guard, such as openarm_v2, keeps only its own arms, grippers and body apart. A robot without one, such as so101, checks no collision at all. An arm move completes only once the measured arm has settled within the tolerances the call names, at or after the planned end of the motion, and fails with timeout once the call's settle timeout runs out, its message giving the remaining error; a posture move completes once every arm stands at the posture, within the robot's own tolerance, and fails with timeout once the robot's settle timeout runs out, its message naming the arm and how far it stands; a gripper move completes once its jaws stand still, or at its settle timeout wherever they stand. An arm can stop short of its goal, against an obstacle or where a robot's collision guard holds it: the move then fails with timeout, its message says how far off the arm stands, and the robot.limb_state resource gives where it ended. Posture result: robot.move_to_ready and robot.move_to_home answer with frame, timestamp, arm_names and grasp_points. grasp_points holds the grasp-point pose of each arm, a position and an orientation, in the order of arm_names, in frame, the robot frame, at timestamp, when the robot measured them as the move completed or a cancel or robot.stop ended it. After a cancel or robot.stop, an arm that was still moving can come to rest away from the pose they give. When the robot has no fresh measured pose of an arm to report, arm_names and grasp_points are empty, frame is the robot frame, and timestamp is the time of the check that found no fresh pose. robot.move_to_home closes every gripper of the robot before its arms move: a gripper that holds an item closes on it, with the effort limit of its last command, a gripper that reports no state gets no command, and a cancel, robot.stop or a failure during the close ends the move before the arms move. robot.move_to_ready sends no command to the grippers, so each gripper goes on as its last gripper command drives it. To stop a robot, call robot.stop; brain.abort ends a brain sequence between its moves. When you find a robot in a state you did not command, call robot.recent_calls: it lists the last state-changing calls of this endpoint with the client that made each, so you can tell whether another client did it. Each move's deadline covers the longest duration and settle timeout a robot admits, 60 s each: 125 s for robot.move_arm, robot.move_arm_joints, robot.execute_joint_path, robot.move_base and robot.drive_base, whose 60 s hold, its ramps and its settle timeout it covers, 65 s for robot.move_gripper and robot.move_to_ready, and 75 s for robot.move_to_home, which also covers the close of the grippers, at most 10 s. navigator.navigate_to_pose, navigator.spin, navigator.drive_on_heading and navigator.back_up wait while the goal reports, however long it runs: the navigator reports at least once a second, and a call that gets no report for 60 s fails, the goal running on until it ends or robot.stop ends it. A duration_s of 0 means as fast as the joint limits allow, or a base's limits for robot.move_base; robot.drive_base takes a hold_duration_s above 0 and refuses 0 with rejected. Every robot refuses with rejected a duration or settle timeout above 60 s. On a robot with a brain, brain.grab_item, brain.place_item and brain.drop_item run whole sequences on its arms through the same backbone the direct moves use; prefer them when the robot has a brain and use the direct moves for a single deliberate motion, since a backbone refuses a second motion on a limb that is moving whichever surface asked. The camera setters drive the device's controls in its own units and ranges, which camera_profile.get reports where the camera implements it; a control the device does not support is refused, a refused request changes nothing, and every setter answers with the effective value after the call, so read the response rather than assuming the request took.",
  },

  // The record of the state-changing calls this endpoint takes: every call
  // of a tool that is not read-only, tasks included, the last 200 of them.
  call_record: {
    tool: "robot.recent_calls",
    description: "List the last calls of this endpoint that could change a robot's state, newest first, up to 200: every mutating tool and every action, with started_at, the client that made the call (its declared name and version, or null), the tool, its arguments as sent (null when larger than 1 kB, with arguments_bytes), outcome (running, completed, failed, cancelled or refused), failure_kind, message and duration_ms. failure_kind is the failure kind the robot gave a refused or failed call, and null when it gave none. message says what ended a call that did not complete: the robot's message, the reason of a cancel, or the words of this endpoint for a call that never reached the robot or did not end there; it is null for a running or completed call. The record holds the calls of MCP clients on this endpoint only; a motion a teleoperation or another node commanded is not in it, so when no entry accounts for a state, no client of this endpoint caused it. Takes no arguments and changes nothing.",
    keep: 200,
  },

  robots: {
    list: {
      tool: "robot.list",
      description: "List every robot of the stack. Each entry carries robot, its name, which every other tool takes; tools, the tools that robot answers, each called with its name; resources, the resources it publishes, each read at peppy://resource/<name>; members, the camera names its camera tools take, under camera for color cameras and depth_camera for cameras that carry depth, and under motor_health and alerts the motor-bearing components whose health and alerts it publishes as resources; identity, its model and the machine hosting it; limbs, its arm and gripper names with the joints behind each arm; and notes, anything that could not be read for it. Takes no arguments and changes nothing.",
    },
    describe: {
      identity: { target: "identity", service: "get_identity" },
      limbs: { target: "limb_state", service: "get_limb_names" },
    },
  },

  targets: {
    // Who each robot is: its initializer answers, the node that works the
    // name out and joins a simulation under it, so the name reported here
    // is the one a simulation lists the robot under.
    identity: {
      contract: {
        name: "robot_identity",
        tag: "v1",
        // An author pin: the sha256 of the exact contract document this file
        // was written against (`sha256sum robot/robot_identity.json5` in
        // the contracts hub). A launch that resolves other bytes refuses,
        // naming the contract. Every pin below is the same kind.
        sha256: "bba017c941f2405be5089acf90d6848f415123b0fe1003d22bf4f0f0a4abd7e8",
      },
      services: [
        {
          member: "get_identity",
          tool: "robot.get_identity",
          description: "Report who the robot is: robot is the name it stands under everywhere, model its model id, for example openarm_v2 or so101, and core_node the machine hosting it. robot.list reports the same under identity. Changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 2000,
        },
      ],
    },

    // The robot's guide: the park poses its model's description gives, which
    // no other contract gives, so a client that drives robots of many
    // models reads them from each robot and keeps no list of models.
    guide: {
      contract: {
        name: "robot_guide",
        tag: "v1",
        sha256: "3156f76bbb15cd4ccb433d352f89225a9bfb6e3d0287e25ed040548329597a00",
      },
      services: [
        {
          member: "get_guide",
          tool: "robot.get_guide",
          description: "Report the robot's guide: the park pose of each arm that has one, as the description of the robot's model gives it. park_arm_names, park_joints_per_arm and park_joint_positions give the joints of each park pose in radians, in the arm's joint order, each arm's vector concatenated in park_arm_names order. An arm at its park pose stands up, above the surface it works on and out of the view of the perception camera, so its next move down to the surface comes from above, over what stands on it; move an arm to its park pose with robot.move_arm_joints. An arm with no park pose is absent from the arrays, and a robot whose description gives none answers empty arrays. Changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 2000,
        },
      ],
    },

    // Whole-robot moves: the backbone's. An action becomes a tool. For a
    // client that declares the MCP tasks extension the call returns a task
    // handle, `tasks/get` reports its progress and `tasks/cancel` forwards
    // to the action's cancel path; for any other client the goal runs
    // inside the call, which answers with the terminal result. Neither
    // contract declares a feedback topic, so a move reports its state
    // transitions and the terminal result, not interim progress.
    postures: {
      contract: {
        name: "postures",
        tag: "v1",
        sha256: "e8fdb8d964fa95fcc23d657786574921cecab95dc23e6c460d674ec9f7afdea7",
      },
      actions: [
        {
          member: "move_to_ready",
          tool: "robot.move_to_ready",
          description: "Bring every arm of the robot to its working posture, raised out in front of it. It sends no command to the grippers. duration_s is the requested move time in seconds. After the call, the result reports where each arm ended in frame, timestamp, arm_names and grasp_points, as the posture result of this endpoint's instructions says.",
          operation: "long_running",
          // Advertised to the client: this one moves the robot.
          safety_sensitive: true,
          deadline_ms: 65000,
        },
        {
          member: "move_to_home",
          tool: "robot.move_to_home",
          description: "Bring every arm of the robot to its rest posture, lower and closer to the robot's body than at its working posture. Its path sees nothing of the scene, so call it only where nothing stands between the arms and their rest posture. duration_s is the requested time of the arm move in seconds. After the call, the result reports where each arm ended in frame, timestamp, arm_names and grasp_points, as the posture result of this endpoint's instructions says. It closes every gripper of the robot before the arms move, and a gripper that holds an item closes on it with the effort limit of its last command.",
          operation: "long_running",
          safety_sensitive: true,
          // The close of the grippers, at most 10 s by the postures
          // contract, then the arm move and its settle.
          deadline_ms: 75000,
        },
      ],
    },

    // One limb at a time: the backbone's. The arm and gripper names are the
    // robot's own, which robot.list reports under limbs.
    limb_motion: {
      contract: {
        name: "limb_motion",
        tag: "v1",
        sha256: "e1b4cc4a24e356455d9b75a921276cea40dc853ef43d965ae2a99f0ef86df956",
      },
      actions: [
        {
          member: "move_arm",
          tool: "robot.move_arm",
          description: "Move one arm's grasp point to a pose, or to a position with no particular orientation. arm_name is one of the robot's arms as robot.list reports them; target is the pose: frame and timestamp, which the robot places by the frame rule when it takes the goal, position [x, y, z] in meters and orientation a unit quaternion [x, y, z, w], left out to name none, when the grasp point aims to keep the turn it starts with. A target in a link of the robot with no timestamp names where that link stands when the robot takes the goal, and a target in a link that the move moves is held there. duration_s is the requested move time in seconds, 0 for the fastest the joint limits allow. The grasp point travels on a straight line, and the plan ends within 1 mm and 0.01 rad of the goal or the move fails with unreachable. A five-joint arm such as so101 ends on few orientations exactly, so leave its orientation out unless the orientation came from where its arm has stood, or, where the robot has a motion planner, reach the pose with planner.plan_joint_path, whose orientation constraint takes slack in tolerance_rad or free_axis, and robot.execute_joint_path. settle_position_tolerance_m says how close the measured grasp point must come to the position for the move to complete, and settle_orientation_tolerance_rad how close the measured orientation must come to the target's or, for a target with no orientation, to where the plan ends: 0.02 and 0.15 are usual. settle_timeout_s is how long after the planned end of the motion the grasp point may still stand outside those before the move fails: 2 is usual, 60 the most. contact_gap_m is the contact check, for a move that sets a held item down or presses on a surface: the farthest, in meters, the measured grasp point may stand from the grasp point the arm is commanded to. An arm that an object holds on its way falls behind its command, and once its grasp point stands farther behind than the gap, the move ends there and completes with ended_on_contact true. 0 is no contact check, the value for every other move. A free arm trails its command too while it moves, by more the faster it moves, so name a gap above that distance and a duration_s that keeps the move slow. Every tolerance is a positive number, every time a number of seconds from 0 to 60, and the gap 0 or a positive number; other values are refused with rejected. The call answers once the arm has settled, has met a contact or the timeout ran out: it completes when the measured grasp point stands within the settle tolerances of the pose, or on a contact, and fails with timeout otherwise, with a message such as 'still 8.0 mm / 0.02 rad from the target, 2.0 s after the planned end'. After the call, the arm holds where it stands either way, and the result of a completed or cancelled move gives final_pose, where the grasp point was when the move ended, in the robot frame at the time the robot measured its joints then, and ended_on_contact, true when a contact ended the move short of the pose. A target the robot cannot place is refused with a kind of the frame rule before the arm moves. A goal no straight line reaches fails with unreachable, and a line the joint limits cannot run within 60 s fails with rejected: call robot.check_arm_move first to learn whether the pose has a plan, and where a robot has a motion planner, plan a joint path to reach what a line does not.",
          operation: "long_running",
          safety_sensitive: true,
          deadline_ms: 125000,
        },
        {
          member: "move_arm_joints",
          tool: "robot.move_arm_joints",
          description: "Move one arm to a joint configuration. arm_name is one of the robot's arms as robot.list reports them; joint_positions holds one value in radians per joint of that arm, as many as robot.list reports for it under limbs, in the arm's fixed joint order: the order of final_joint_positions in the result, and of the arm's joint_positions in the robot.limb_state resource; duration_s is the requested move time in seconds, 0 for the fastest the joint limits allow. settle_tolerances_rad holds one positive value per joint, in the same order: how close each measured joint must come to its target for the move to complete, 0.1 each being usual. settle_timeout_s is how long after the planned end of the motion a joint may still stand outside its tolerance before the move fails: 2 is usual, 60 the most. contact_gap_m is the contact check, as robot.move_arm has it: the farthest, in meters, the measured grasp point of the arm may stand from the grasp point of the joints it is commanded to; once it stands farther, the move ends there and completes with ended_on_contact true, and 0 is no contact check. The call answers once the arm has settled, has met a contact or the timeout ran out: it fails with timeout when a joint is still off, with a message naming the joint and by how much. After the call, the arm holds where it stands either way, and the result of a completed or cancelled move gives final_joint_positions, the joints the robot measured when the move ended, and ended_on_contact, true when a contact ended the move short of the target. A vector of another length, a value more than 0.001 rad outside its joint limits (the message names the joint, its value and its limits), a tolerance that is not positive, or a negative contact gap, is refused; a value closer to its limits runs at the limit, and a move the joint limits cannot run within 60 s fails with rejected. The way to use it: pass back the final_joint_positions of an earlier robot.move_arm_joints of the arm, to return to that configuration.",
          operation: "long_running",
          safety_sensitive: true,
          deadline_ms: 125000,
        },
        {
          member: "execute_joint_path",
          tool: "robot.execute_joint_path",
          description: "Move one or more arms through joint waypoints without stopping at the inner ones, all on one timing: the arms pass their k-th waypoints together and come to rest at the last ones. Use it to run several waypoints in one move; for one arm to one configuration, robot.move_arm_joints is enough. arms lists the arms to move, each once, by the arm names robot.list reports: each with waypoints, the configurations to pass through after where the arm stands when the move starts (the robot moves the arm's command onto its measured joints, clamped into the joint limits, before the path runs), every arm with as many, each waypoint's joint_positions holding one value in radians per joint of that arm, as many as robot.list reports for it under limbs, in the arm's fixed joint order; expected_start_joint_positions, empty for no check, or the joints the arm stood at when the path was made, which then fails the move with precondition_failed before any arm moves unless each measured joint stands within the robot's start tolerance of it, the message naming the joint; and settle_tolerances_rad, one positive value per joint of the arm, how close each measured joint must come to the arm's last waypoint, 0.1 each being usual. The arms run straight in joint space toward each waypoint in turn and cut each inner corner on an arc whose midpoint lies at most 0.1 from the waypoint. They stop at their last waypoints, where the path turns back on itself (a turn of about 179.74 degrees or more, the cosine of the angle between one leg and the next at -0.99999 or less), and where every arm repeats its waypoint before, within 0.001 in every joint. duration_s is the requested time of the whole path in seconds, 0 for the fastest the joint limits allow; settle_timeout_s is how long after the planned end a joint may still stand outside its tolerance before the move fails: 2 is usual, 60 the most; contact_gap_m is the contact check, as robot.move_arm has it, one value for every arm of the move: the farthest, in meters, the measured grasp point of an arm may stand from the grasp point of the joints that arm is commanded to; once one arm's stands farther, the move ends for every arm and completes, and 0 is no contact check. The call answers once the arms have run the path to its end and settled, an arm has met a contact, or the timeout ran out: it fails with timeout when a joint is still off, the message naming the joint and by how much (still 0.120 rad from the target at joint 3, 2.0 s after the planned end), or, with every joint within its tolerance of the last waypoint, when the guard held the path back short of its end (still short of the end of its path, 2.0 s after the planned end); on a move of two arms the message leads with the arm's name (left_arm: still short of the end of its path, 2.0 s after the planned end). On a robot with a collision guard, a path its guard would hold short, with every other limb where it stands at the start, fails with unreachable before any arm moves, the robot checking it at points at most 0.01 rad apart and where it rests; a limb that moves during the path can still make the guard hold it short, and the move then fails with timeout. A path the joint limits cannot run within 60 s fails with rejected before any arm moves, and the message gives its time. An arm whose measured joints move farther than the robot's start tolerance from where they stood while the robot checks the path fails the move with precondition_failed before any arm moves, and the message says to send the path again; on a robot with a collision guard, which checks the path again from where an arm that moved more than 0.001 rad stands, so does an arm that still moves after 20 checks, the message saying to let it settle. An arm measured outside a joint limit when the robot takes the path is held inside that limit, whether or not the path then runs. After the call, the arms hold where they stand either way, and the result of a completed or cancelled move gives, for each arm, final_joint_positions, the joints the robot measured when the move ended, and ended_on_contact, true for the arm whose contact ended the move. An unknown or repeated arm, arms with different numbers of waypoints, a vector of another length, a waypoint value more than 0.001 rad outside its joint limits (a value closer runs at the limit), a tolerance that is not positive, or a negative contact gap, is refused.",
          operation: "long_running",
          safety_sensitive: true,
          deadline_ms: 125000,
        },
        {
          member: "move_gripper",
          tool: "robot.move_gripper",
          description: "Drive one gripper to an opening. gripper_name is one of the robot's grippers as robot.list reports them; opening is a fraction of full jaw travel, 0 closed and 1 fully open. max_effort caps the grip in the gripper's own effort unit, 0 meaning no cap from you; it can only lower the gripper's own limit, and some grippers, such as the so101's, ignore it. settle_timeout_s is the longest the call waits, from the planned end of the commanded travel on, for the jaws to stand still: 2 is usual, 60 the most, and an openarm_v2's jaws stand still about 1 s after the commanded travel. After the call, the gripper keeps driving toward the opening it was sent to, so it keeps squeezing what it holds, until the next gripper move or a leader that streams to the gripper. The call completes with settled true as soon as the jaws stand still, on the opening or stopped short of it by an item or the effort cap, and at the latest at settle_timeout_s, wherever the jaws stand, with settled false: a gripper move never fails on its settle. final_opening is the opening measured when the call answers, and the result reports no force; read final_opening to judge a grasp.",
          operation: "long_running",
          safety_sensitive: true,
          deadline_ms: 65000,
        },
      ],
      services: [
        {
          member: "check_arm_move",
          tool: "robot.check_arm_move",
          description: "Learn whether a robot.move_arm goal with these fields has a plan from where the arm is commanded now, without moving anything. The fields are arm_name, target and duration_s of robot.move_arm, with the same meaning: the robot places target by the frame rule, at its frame and its timestamp, when it takes the request. Answers with reachable, true when a plan exists; duration_s, the time the move would take, 0 when not reachable; and reason, why no plan exists, in the words that the robot.move_arm failure gives, empty when reachable. A request that robot.move_arm would refuse is refused with the kind of that refusal: rejected for an unknown arm or a value robot.move_arm does not take, precondition_failed while the arm is executing a move, which the check does not disturb, leader_streaming while a leader streams to the arm, and a kind of the frame rule for a target the robot cannot place. The check finds a plan and no more: it does not check collisions with the robot itself or with the room, so a move that passes the check can still stop short. Changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
      ],
