    // The robot stop: the robot's backbone ends every planned move of the
    // robot and brings its mobile base to rest.
    robot_stop: {
      contract: {
        name: "robot_stop",
        tag: "v1",
        sha256: "d99d5599bdec7a1735ee3fe92927002bf7478187bffd0163a42a93b99af3eca2",
      },
      services: [
        {
          member: "stop",
          tool: "robot.stop",
          description: "Stop the robot: end every planned move in flight on it, whoever started it, the brain's moves included, and bring its mobile base to rest. Each move ends cancelled, with a reason that names the stop and the reason this call gives, which may be empty; a move accepted that has not started to move ends too, and a move that a cancel ended before keeps the reason of that cancel. A navigator goal ends through the base: cancelled, with a reason that names the robot stop, or with resource_unavailable when the stop comes during a silence of the base's motion sensing. After the call, each arm whose move the stop ended holds where it stands, each gripper holds where it was commanded to be when the stop came, and no gripper opens, so a held item stays held. A mobile base comes to rest at its deceleration limits and is held at rest against the leader that streams velocities to it on drive_link until 0.5 s pass, from the stop on, with no velocity from that leader. It does not stop a leader that streams setpoints to the robot's limbs, such as a teleoperation. Answers with stopped, the parts whose move it ended and the base when its leader was streaming to it, by the names the robot's tools give them, sorted by name, empty when there were none. The stop fails with attempt_failed when the robot cannot carry it out within its answer time, the message saying why; a stop the robot took before that reply still runs. A move sent after the stop runs, except a base move that the base's hold refuses, as robot.drive_base and robot.move_base state, and a navigator goal, which the hold refuses as navigator.navigate_to_pose states.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 2000,
        },
      ],
    },

    // A mobile base's moves: its backbone's. Each action becomes a tool
    // that runs as a task for a client that declares the tasks extension,
    // and inside the call for any other; move_base's feedback reports how far
    // the base stands from its target as the move runs.
    base_motion: {
      contract: {
        name: "base_motion",
        tag: "v1",
        sha256: "1d0aaae5f2426dfbd9a7f98da82958bc1cc10444f879c61e382f3b67f0d2b716",
      },
      actions: [
        {
          member: "drive_base",
          tool: "robot.drive_base",
          description: "Drive the robot's mobile base at one velocity for a time, then bring it to rest. velocity is the velocity of the robot frame: frame and timestamp, which the robot places in the robot frame by the frame rule when it takes the goal, linear_velocity [x, y, z] in m/s and angular_velocity [x, y, z] in rad/s; the base then drives that velocity in the robot frame as it moves. In the robot frame, linear_velocity is x forward, negative to drive backward, y leftward, negative to drive rightward, and z 0, x and y each within the min_velocity and max_velocity that robot.describe_base reports in linear_limits for that axis, so y is 0 on a differential base, and angular_velocity is [0, 0, z], z the turn rate counterclockwise seen from above, within the min_rate and max_rate of angular_limits. An axis commanded slower than its deadband, which robot.describe_base reports, does not move. hold_duration_s is how long the base holds the velocity from the moment it reaches it, above 0 and at most 60. settle_timeout_s, 0 to 60, is how long after the command reaches 0 the base may take to come to rest before the drive fails with timeout, the message giving the base's velocity then: 2 is usual; rest reads 0.2 s of robot.base_state, so below 0.2 the drive fails unless the base already rests when the command reaches 0. The base ramps to the velocity at its acceleration limits and back to rest at its deceleration limits, and checks no obstacle on the way. The drive completes once the base rests, as robot.base_state reports rest. A call that its caller cancels, or that robot.stop ends, brings the base to rest and ends cancelled once the base rests, or once settle_timeout_s has passed since the cancel or the stop, whichever comes first. After the call, the base rests where it stands, unless the call failed, after which it comes to rest at its deceleration limits, or a cancelled call ended at its settle timeout before the base came to rest. The result of a completed or cancelled drive gives final_pose, where the base's odometry placed the robot frame when the drive ended: frame, odom, timestamp, the time of that pose, position [x, y, 0] in meters and orientation, a unit quaternion that turns about +z alone; and action_time, how long the call took in seconds. A call is checked in this order: a time outside its range or a value that is not finite is refused with rejected; a velocity the robot cannot place, with a kind of the frame rule; a velocity that, placed in the robot frame, has a z of linear_velocity, a y of linear_velocity on a differential base, or an x or y of angular_velocity farther than 1e-9 from 0, with rejected, the message saying to send [vx, vy, 0] and [0, 0, wz] in the robot frame, vy 0 on a differential base, and the robot takes each of them within 1e-9 of 0 as 0; a velocity whose x or y of linear_velocity, placed in the robot frame, lies outside the min_velocity and max_velocity of linear_limits, or whose z of angular_velocity lies outside the min_rate and max_rate of angular_limits, with rejected; while the base's motion sensing is silent, before the base first reports and once its newest measurement is more than 0.5 s old, with resource_unavailable: retry once the base reports its odometry; while the base's mode on robot.base_state is neither standing nor moving, with precondition_failed, the message naming the mode and what brings the base to standing; while a leader streams to the base on drive_link, with leader_streaming: retry once the leader stops streaming; and while another base move runs or the base still comes to rest from one, or a move of the robot's other parts holds the base, with precondition_failed: wait for that move to end, then retry. A call fails with resource_unavailable when the base's motion sensing falls silent, when its odometry starts over and when the base's clock goes back during it, the base coming to rest, and with attempt_failed when the base's mode leaves standing and moving, the message naming the mode. A leader that starts streaming during the call does not move the base: the base drops its velocities until the call ends.",
          operation: "long_running",
          safety_sensitive: true,
          deadline_ms: 125000,
        },
        {
          member: "move_base",
          tool: "robot.move_base",
          description: "Move the robot's mobile base to a pose near it and bring it to rest there. target is the pose to move the robot frame to: frame and timestamp, which the robot places in odom by the frame rule when it takes the goal, position [x, y, z] in meters and orientation a unit quaternion [x, y, z, w]. In odom, the position's z is 0 and the orientation turns about +z alone, [0, 0, sin(yaw/2), cos(yaw/2)] for a yaw counterclockwise seen from above, turned the shorter way. A target in the robot frame with no timestamp names a pose from where the robot stands when it takes the goal: position [1.2, 0, 0] with orientation [0, 0, 0, 1] drives 1.2 m ahead, and position [0, 0, 0] with orientation [0, 0, 0.7071, 0.7071] turns a quarter turn to the left. duration_s is the requested move time in seconds, 0 to 60, 0 for the fastest the base's limits allow; a shorter time than the limits allow is lengthened to it. settle_position_tolerance_m and settle_orientation_tolerance_rad say how close the robot frame must come to the target for the move to complete: 0.02 and 0.03 are usual, and a settle_orientation_tolerance_rad of pi or more leaves the heading free. settle_timeout_s, 0 to 60, is how long after the planned end of the motion the base may take to come to rest within both tolerances before the move fails with timeout, its message saying how far the base stands from the target: 2 is usual, and with 0 the move fails at its planned end unless the base already rests within them. A differential base turns toward the target, drives to it and turns to the target's heading, and turns in place when the target lies within settle_position_tolerance_m of where it stood when the robot took the goal; an omnidirectional base drives straight to the target while it turns to the target's heading. The base steers by its odometry, so the pose is as exact as that odometry, and checks no obstacle on the way. While the move runs, its progress gives distance_remaining_m in meters and angle_remaining_rad in radians, from 0 to pi, how far the base stands from the target. A call that its caller cancels, or that robot.stop ends, brings the base to rest and ends cancelled once the base rests, or once settle_timeout_s has passed since the cancel or the stop, whichever comes first. After the call, the base rests where it stands, unless the call failed, after which it comes to rest at its deceleration limits, or a cancelled call ended at its settle timeout before the base came to rest. The result of a completed or cancelled move gives final_pose, where the base's odometry placed the robot frame when the move ended: frame, odom, timestamp, the time of that pose, position [x, y, 0] in meters and orientation, a unit quaternion that turns about +z alone; and action_time, how long the call took in seconds. A call is checked in this order: a tolerance that is not above 0, a time outside its range or a value that is not finite is refused with rejected; a target the robot cannot place, with a kind of the frame rule, and before the base first reports, while its tree holds no odom, with unknown_frame: retry once robot.base_state reports; a target that, placed in odom, has a z farther than 1e-9 m from 0 or turns by more than 1e-9 rad about an axis other than +z, with rejected, the message naming the part, its value in odom and what to send, a target in odom or the robot frame with a position [x, y, 0] and an orientation [0, 0, sin(yaw/2), cos(yaw/2)]; a move the base's limits cannot run within 60 s, with rejected; while the base's motion sensing is silent, once its newest measurement is more than 0.5 s old, with resource_unavailable: retry once the base reports its odometry; while the base's mode on robot.base_state is neither standing nor moving, with precondition_failed, the message naming the mode and what brings the base to standing; while a leader streams to the base on drive_link, with leader_streaming: retry once the leader stops streaming; and while another base move runs or the base still comes to rest from one, or a move of the robot's other parts holds the base, with precondition_failed: wait for that move to end, then retry. A call fails with resource_unavailable when the base's motion sensing falls silent, when its odometry starts over and when the base's clock goes back during it, the base coming to rest, and with attempt_failed when the base's mode leaves standing and moving, the message naming the mode. A leader that starts streaming during the call does not move the base: the base drops its velocities until the call ends.",
          operation: "long_running",
          safety_sensitive: true,
          deadline_ms: 125000,
        },
      ],
    },

    // What a mobile base is and where its odometry places it: its backbone
    // answers the base's odometry source, the limits, deadband and rest
    // velocity of each axis, and its outline on demand, and streams the
    // odometry as a resource per robot.
    base_state: {
      contract: {
        name: "base_state",
        tag: "v1",
        sha256: "f21e15ddc9f203084e600302fc5d4a0b5f00e06854762cb527f5cc9668e5f856",
      },
      topics: [
        {
          member: "base_states",
          resource: "robot.base_state",
          description: "The latest measurement of the robot's mobile base, as nav_msgs/Odometry gives it: pose, where its odometry places the robot frame in frame, odom, a position [x, y, z] in meters with z 0 and an orientation, a unit quaternion that turns about +z alone; twist, the velocity the base executes in child_frame, the robot frame base_footprint, linear_velocity [x, y, z] in m/s with y 0 on a differential base and z 0, and angular_velocity [x, y, z] in rad/s, its z the turn rate counterclockwise seen from above and its x and y 0; mode, what the base can do now, off, damped, posed, transition, upright, standing, moving or fallen, a wheeled base giving off, damped, standing or moving, and the base taking velocity while standing or moving; parts_within_footprint, true while every limb stands within the footprint robot.describe_base reports, and on a robot with no limb; odometry_start_timestamp, when odom was fixed, on the wall clock of the base's machine, a new value meaning the odometry started over at zero; and timestamp, the time of the measurement on the robot's clock, the simulation clock in a simulation. The odometry drifts as the base slips. The base rests, its mode standing, while in every state of the last 0.2 s each axis of the velocity lies within its rest bound of robot.describe_base, the bound included. The base publishes no state while its sensing of its motion is silent, from 0.5 s after its newest measurement, and the resource goes stale 2 s after its last state.",
          freshness: { max_age_ms: 2000 },
          update: { max_hz: 5 },
          // The frame names are strings, so the runtime size policy is
          // mandatory; a base state is small, and one that is not is a fault
          // to refuse.
          max_result_bytes: 4096,
          on_oversize: "reject",
        },
      ],
      services: [
        {
          member: "describe_base",
          tool: "robot.describe_base",
          description: "Report what the robot's mobile base is and how fast it may move, whatever it is doing: base_name, its part name, which robot.stop answers in stopped; odometry_source, measured when the base senses its own motion, such as with wheel encoders, and commanded when its odometry integrates the velocities it executes; linear_limits, the limits of its velocity along x, forward, and y, leftward, in the robot frame, each an [x, y] pair: min_velocity and max_velocity in m/s, the most negative and the most positive velocity the base executes, 0 to 0 along y on a differential base; max_acceleration and max_deceleration in m/s^2, the fastest the velocity's magnitude may grow and fall; deadband in m/s, the smallest magnitude the base executes alone, a smaller command not moving that axis; and rest_velocity in m/s, the largest magnitude of the measured velocity at which the base counts as at rest; and angular_limits, the same for its turn rate, the z of angular_velocity, counterclockwise seen from above: min_rate and max_rate in rad/s, max_acceleration and max_deceleration in rad/s^2, deadband and rest_rate in rad/s. Every robot.drive_base and robot.move_base keeps the velocity, acceleration and deceleration limits. footprint is the outline on the floor of the base and of every part fixed to it: frame, base_footprint, the robot frame, and points, its corners counterclockwise seen from above, each a position [x, y, 0] in meters; it holds at every time, so it gives no timestamp. The answer holds until the base's backbone restarts, which gives robot.base_state a new odometry_start_timestamp. Changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 2000,
        },
      ],
    },

    // What each robot's limbs are called and where they are: the backbone
